1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:03,519
Summer, 1939.

2
00:00:04,219 --> 00:00:07,063
Across much of Europe, life seems normal.

3
00:00:07,383 --> 00:00:10,108
Within weeks, Europe
will be at war, in what

4
00:00:10,108 --> 00:00:13,019
will become the deadliest
war in human history.

5
00:00:13,519 --> 00:00:15,100
Countries will fall.

6
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:16,682
Cities will burn.

7
00:00:16,932 --> 00:00:18,984
Tens of millions will die.

8
00:00:19,684 --> 00:00:21,104
So how did it start?

9
00:00:21,404 --> 00:00:22,765
And how did it end?

10
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:35,128
To understand the Second World War,
we have to start with the First.

11
00:00:35,448 --> 00:00:41,092
In November 1918, Germany's army was
beaten, and its emperor had abdicated.

12
00:00:41,412 --> 00:00:44,562
The following year, the
Treaty of Versailles made

13
00:00:44,562 --> 00:00:47,840
Germany accept responsibility
for the war's damage.

14
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:53,573
It lost around 13 per cent of its European
territory and all of its colonies,

15
00:00:53,573 --> 00:00:59,127
its army was capped at 100,000 men, and
it was ordered to pay huge reparations.

16
00:00:59,447 --> 00:01:01,760
Many Germans felt humiliated.

17
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:06,733
Germany's new democracy, the Weimar
Republic, struggled from the start.

18
00:01:07,053 --> 00:01:11,640
Then, in 1929, the Wall Street Crash
triggered the Great Depression.

19
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:16,115
By 1932, around six million
Germans were out of work.

20
00:01:16,615 --> 00:01:19,590
But Versailles alone did
not cause another war.

21
00:01:19,910 --> 00:01:24,359
Economic despair, fragile politics,
nationalism and antisemitism

22
00:01:24,359 --> 00:01:28,530
created an opening, and extremists
were ready to exploit it.

23
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:35,093
One of those extremists was Adolf
Hitler, leader of the Nazi Party.

24
00:01:35,413 --> 00:01:39,864
Nazi propaganda offered simple answers
to complicated problems, blaming

25
00:01:39,864 --> 00:01:44,189
Germany's troubles on Jewish people,
communists and the peace treaty.

26
00:01:44,509 --> 00:01:48,410
In January 1933, Hitler
was appointed Chancellor.

27
00:01:48,730 --> 00:01:51,640
Within months, he had banned
rival parties, crushed

28
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:54,721
the free press and turned
Germany into a dictatorship.

29
00:01:55,041 --> 00:01:59,212
Nazi ideology was racist,
antisemitic and expansionist.

30
00:01:59,532 --> 00:02:02,005
Jewish Germans were
stripped of their rights,

31
00:02:02,005 --> 00:02:04,094
while Hitler rebuilt the armed forces.

32
00:02:04,494 --> 00:02:09,276
In 1936, German troops reoccupied
the demilitarised Rhineland.

33
00:02:09,596 --> 00:02:12,962
In March 1938, Germany annexed Austria.

34
00:02:13,282 --> 00:02:17,019
That September, at Munich,
Britain and France let Hitler take

35
00:02:17,019 --> 00:02:20,818
the Sudetenland from Czechoslovakia,
hoping to keep the peace.

36
00:02:21,138 --> 00:02:22,086
It didn't work.

37
00:02:22,386 --> 00:02:25,233
In March 1939, Germany occupied the rest

38
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:28,293
of the Czech lands.
Appeasement had failed.

39
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:35,330
Hitler's next target was Poland.
But first, he made a shocking deal.

40
00:02:35,650 --> 00:02:39,566
In August 1939, Nazi Germany
and the Soviet Union,

41
00:02:39,566 --> 00:02:43,090
bitter enemies, signed
a non-aggression pact.

42
00:02:43,410 --> 00:02:46,884
A secret protocol divided
much of Eastern Europe

43
00:02:46,884 --> 00:02:50,068
into German and Soviet
spheres of influence.

44
00:02:50,568 --> 00:02:55,929
On 1 September 1939, German
forces invaded Poland.

45
00:02:56,249 --> 00:02:58,727
Tanks and aircraft struck with terrifying

46
00:02:58,727 --> 00:03:01,568
speed, though the Polish
army fought back hard.

47
00:03:01,888 --> 00:03:05,783
Two days later, Britain and
France declared war on Germany.

48
00:03:06,103 --> 00:03:08,886
The Second World War in Europe had begun.

49
00:03:09,286 --> 00:03:14,860
Then, on 17 September, the Soviet
Union invaded Poland from the east.

50
00:03:15,180 --> 00:03:17,637
Within weeks, Poland had been carved up.

51
00:03:18,037 --> 00:03:23,306
But war had already come to
Asia. Japan had invaded Manchuria

52
00:03:23,306 --> 00:03:28,575
in 1931, and launched a full-scale
war against China in 1937.

53
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:34,081
For months, the Western
Front was eerily quiet.

54
00:03:34,401 --> 00:03:39,323
Then, in April 1940, Germany
invaded Denmark and Norway.

55
00:03:39,643 --> 00:03:44,027
In May, it struck the Netherlands,
Belgium, Luxembourg and France.

56
00:03:44,347 --> 00:03:47,246
German tanks pushed
through the Ardennes forest

57
00:03:47,246 --> 00:03:50,207
and raced for the coast,
trapping Allied armies.

58
00:03:50,527 --> 00:03:54,503
From Dunkirk, more than
330,000 troops were evacuated

59
00:03:54,503 --> 00:03:58,780
across the Channel, helped by
a flotilla of little ships.

60
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:01,054
But in June, France fell.

61
00:04:01,454 --> 00:04:06,249
Britain's new Prime Minister, Winston
Churchill, refused to make peace.

62
00:04:06,569 --> 00:04:11,596
That summer, in the Battle of Britain, the
Royal Air Force fought off the Luftwaffe.

63
00:04:11,916 --> 00:04:15,619
From September, Germany turned
to bombing British cities.

64
00:04:15,619 --> 00:04:18,997
More than 40,000 civilians
were killed in the Blitz.

65
00:04:19,317 --> 00:04:20,999
But Britain kept fighting.

66
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:34,733
On 22 June 1941, Hitler launched Operation

67
00:04:34,733 --> 00:04:38,946
Barbarossa, the invasion
of the Soviet Union.

68
00:04:39,266 --> 00:04:42,411
More than three million
Axis soldiers poured

69
00:04:42,411 --> 00:04:45,555
across a front nearly
3,000 kilometres long.

70
00:04:45,875 --> 00:04:51,249
The Eastern Front would become the largest
and deadliest theatre of the entire war.

71
00:04:51,569 --> 00:04:54,598
German forces reached
the outskirts of Moscow,

72
00:04:54,598 --> 00:04:57,692
but as winter set in,
the Red Army struck back.

73
00:04:58,092 --> 00:05:02,632
Meanwhile, Japan was expanding
across East and South-East Asia.

74
00:05:02,952 --> 00:05:07,336
On 7 December 1941, Japanese
aircraft attacked the

75
00:05:07,336 --> 00:05:11,456
American naval base at
Pearl Harbor, in Hawaii.

76
00:05:11,776 --> 00:05:15,221
More than 2,400 Americans were killed.

77
00:05:15,541 --> 00:05:18,847
The United States declared
war on Japan. Days

78
00:05:18,847 --> 00:05:22,447
later, Germany declared
war on the United States.

79
00:05:22,767 --> 00:05:25,668
Now the war was truly
global, fought across

80
00:05:25,668 --> 00:05:28,164
Europe, Africa, Asia and the Pacific.

81
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:34,147
In 1942, the tide began to turn.

82
00:05:34,466 --> 00:05:39,269
In June, at the Battle of Midway,
American forces sank four Japanese

83
00:05:39,269 --> 00:05:44,355
aircraft carriers, a blow from which
Japan's navy never fully recovered.

84
00:05:44,675 --> 00:05:48,482
In North Africa, British and
Commonwealth forces under

85
00:05:48,482 --> 00:05:52,077
General Montgomery defeated
the Axis at El Alamein.

86
00:05:52,397 --> 00:05:55,727
And at Stalingrad, on the
River Volga, the Red Army

87
00:05:55,727 --> 00:05:59,449
encircled more than a quarter
of a million Axis soldiers.

88
00:05:59,769 --> 00:06:03,198
In February 1943, the
survivors surrendered.

89
00:06:03,198 --> 00:06:06,393
It was a catastrophic defeat for Germany.

90
00:06:06,793 --> 00:06:09,156
No single battle won the war.

91
00:06:09,476 --> 00:06:12,871
But the Allies were also
winning a battle of production.

92
00:06:13,191 --> 00:06:16,938
American, Soviet and British
factories turned out tanks,

93
00:06:16,938 --> 00:06:20,751
aircraft and ships faster than
the Axis could ever match.

94
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:35,826
By 1944, the Allies were ready
to open a new front in the west.

95
00:06:36,146 --> 00:06:39,569
On 6 June, Operation Overlord began.

96
00:06:39,889 --> 00:06:44,389
In the largest seaborne invasion
in history, around 156,000

97
00:06:44,389 --> 00:06:48,813
American, British, Canadian
and other Allied troops landed

98
00:06:48,813 --> 00:06:53,313
in Normandy, from the sea on
five beaches and from the air.

99
00:06:53,633 --> 00:06:57,278
Thousands were killed or
wounded on the first day alone,

100
00:06:57,278 --> 00:07:00,793
but by nightfall, the Allies
had a foothold in France.

101
00:07:01,113 --> 00:07:04,120
As they fought their
way inland, resistance

102
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:07,267
fighters sabotaged
railways and supply lines.

103
00:07:07,587 --> 00:07:11,204
In August 1944, Paris was liberated.

104
00:07:11,604 --> 00:07:16,839
Meanwhile, in the east, a huge Soviet
offensive smashed through German lines.

105
00:07:17,159 --> 00:07:20,016
Germany was now fighting
on several fronts,

106
00:07:20,016 --> 00:07:22,541
and running out of men, fuel and time.

107
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:36,122
In 1945, Allied armies closed
in on Germany from both sides.

108
00:07:36,442 --> 00:07:40,598
As they advanced, they uncovered
the full horror of Nazi rule.

109
00:07:41,098 --> 00:07:44,548
In the concentration and
death camps, soldiers found

110
00:07:44,548 --> 00:07:47,931
starving survivors and the
evidence of mass murder.

111
00:07:48,431 --> 00:07:53,058
In the Holocaust, Nazi Germany and
its collaborators systematically

112
00:07:53,058 --> 00:07:57,132
murdered around six million
Jewish men, women and children.

113
00:07:57,732 --> 00:08:02,414
Millions of others were also
persecuted and killed, including Roma,

114
00:08:02,414 --> 00:08:06,747
disabled people, Polish civilians
and Soviet prisoners of war.

115
00:08:07,447 --> 00:08:10,819
In April, Soviet troops
fought their way into Berlin.

116
00:08:11,139 --> 00:08:16,789
On 30 April 1945, Hitler took
his own life in his bunker.

117
00:08:17,109 --> 00:08:20,984
Days later, Germany surrendered
unconditionally, and

118
00:08:20,984 --> 00:08:24,710
on 8 May, crowds celebrated
Victory in Europe Day.

119
00:08:25,110 --> 00:08:27,455
But in the Pacific, the war went on.

120
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:36,779
By 1945, American forces were closing
in on Japan, island by island.

121
00:08:37,099 --> 00:08:40,343
The Battle of Okinawa, from April to June,

122
00:08:40,343 --> 00:08:44,050
killed well over 100,000
soldiers and civilians.

123
00:08:44,370 --> 00:08:47,474
Japan's leaders still
refused to surrender.

124
00:08:47,974 --> 00:08:53,076
On 6 August, the United States
dropped an atomic bomb on Hiroshima.

125
00:08:53,396 --> 00:08:56,008
Tens of thousands of
people died instantly,

126
00:08:56,008 --> 00:08:58,560
and many more in the months that followed.

127
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:02,977
On 8 August, the Soviet
Union declared war on Japan,

128
00:09:02,977 --> 00:09:06,744
and within hours its armies
poured into Manchuria.

129
00:09:07,064 --> 00:09:11,073
The next day, a second atomic
bomb devastated Nagasaki.

130
00:09:11,473 --> 00:09:15,771
On 15 August, Emperor
Hirohito announced Japan's

131
00:09:15,771 --> 00:09:20,874
surrender, which was formally
signed on 2 September 1945.

132
00:09:21,194 --> 00:09:24,447
Historians still debate
how much the bombs and

133
00:09:24,447 --> 00:09:27,913
the Soviet invasion each
shaped Japan's decision.

134
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:35,980
The war killed an estimated 60 million
people or more, most of them civilians.

135
00:09:36,300 --> 00:09:41,379
From the ruins came the United Nations,
and a new rivalry: the Cold War.

136
00:09:41,779 --> 00:09:44,248
The war showed how quickly hatred and

137
00:09:44,248 --> 00:09:47,251
unchecked aggression
can lead to catastrophe.

138
00:09:47,571 --> 00:09:50,660
But it also showed the
courage of ordinary people.

139
00:09:51,060 --> 00:09:54,679
Because history isn't just
about battles or leaders.

140
00:09:54,879 --> 00:09:57,960
It's about people. And
every person has a story.

